<section>
  <h2>Impedance Behavior beyond a Half-Turn</h2>
  <p>
    Exponential coordinates and unit quaternions are widely used to define the orientation error in 6D impedance control. However, the angular
    deflections they represent cannot exceed a half-turn, and consequently, the spring torque reverses its direction beyond that range. A memory-based
    SO(3) representation overcomes this limitation and preserves the spring torque direction.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <a href="{{ '/assets/img/research/memory-based-so3/fig1_teaser.png' | relative_url }}">
      <img
        src="{{ '/assets/img/research/memory-based-so3/fig1_teaser.png' | relative_url }}"
        alt="Conventional and proposed impedance control beyond a half-turn, showing reversed and preserved spring torque directions"
        loading="lazy"
      >
    </a>
    <figcaption>
      Beyond a π-turn, the spring torque reverses its direction under the conventional impedance controller, whereas it maintains its direction under
      the proposed impedance controller.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Extending the Representation through Integration</h2>
  <p>
    The key idea is to extend the representation through integration, leveraging past state information. In the simple SO(2) case, the conventional
    representation becomes discontinuous at ±π, whereas the extended one preserves the actual rotation angle. In SO(3), an observer ensures that the
    observer error between the represented and true rotations converges to zero.
  </p>
  <figure class="research-figure--full">
    <a href="{{ '/assets/img/research/memory-based-so3/fig2_extended_angle_so2.png' | relative_url }}">
      <img
        src="{{ '/assets/img/research/memory-based-so3/fig2_extended_angle_so2.png' | relative_url }}"
        alt="Conventional SO(2) representation discontinuous at ±π compared with the continuous extended rotation angle"
        loading="lazy"
      >
    </a>
    <figcaption>Comparison of the conventional and extended SO(2) representations.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <h3>EEC and EUQ</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Extended exponential coordinates (EEC), ICRA 2022.</strong> EEC extends exponential coordinates from the open π-ball to the entire R³ to
      represent rotations over multiple turns. However, a computational singularity near 2π requires switching among update methods, and the observer
      error increases in that region.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Extended unit quaternion (EUQ), RA-L 2026.</strong> EUQ utilizes the entire unit quaternion space to represent rotations up to a full
      turn without computational singularities.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <figure>
    <a href="{{ '/assets/img/research/memory-based-so3/fig3_sim_euq_vs_eec.png' | relative_url }}">
      <img
        src="{{ '/assets/img/research/memory-based-so3/fig3_sim_euq_vs_eec.png' | relative_url }}"
        alt="EUQ and EEC simulation results near a 2π rotation, with increased EEC observer error in gray shaded regions"
        loading="lazy"
      >
    </a>
    <figcaption>EUQ (left) and EEC (right) near a 2π rotation. The EEC observer error increases in the gray shaded regions.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Experimental Evaluation</h2>
  <p>Two experiments are conducted using a 7-DoF Franka Emika Panda robot with a torque controller operating at 1 kHz.</p>
  <h3>Comparison with the Conventional Controller</h3>
  <p>
    An external torque is applied to the end-effector about its body z-axis until the angular deflection exceeds π. The conventional controller
    reverses the spring torque direction, whereas the EUQ-based controller preserves it and the end-effector converges to its initial orientation
    through the return motion.
  </p>
